      "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of $y=4({2}^{x})+4?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we plot the graph of $y={2}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14220062/assets/6d5b359544227b89d1dc5ca9967c9dfcda84bf5345184063a1d0deef994c3692.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=4({2}^{x}).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $4.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,4),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14220062/assets/86b10c71101ae46d4c99335874eb3cd75abc416c0d9a3b4552f6f6a52644aa2f.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=4({2}^{x})+4.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $4$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,4)$ to $(0,8),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14220062/assets/052daab57bdb99d53853e8d7d2cc7a059f9f24f715b1340857407d5a44c68acd.png)",
